<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-s05e02" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Episode 2: Evaluating Formulas</title>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 2 Learning Goals</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Evaluate given formulas to calculate desired quantities.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 2 Learning Goals</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Evaluate ("plug in")
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <p>
    In the previous episode, we noted that an expression represents a calculation that cannot be complete because it contains an unknown number (a variable).
  </p>
  <definition xml:id="def-evaluating">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <term>Evaluating</term> an expression is completing the calculation after the value of the variable becomes known. In other words, we "plug in" a specific number
        for a variable.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <example>
    <p>
      In the previous episode, we represented the cost of buying a dozen eggs by <m>C</m>, so that the total cost of buying 3 dozen eggs was
      <md>
        3C
      </md>
      You enter the grocery store and find the cost of a dozen eggs is currently $2.18. Use this info to complete the calculation in the expression.
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: Replace the <m>C</m> in the formula with 2.18, so that instead of 3 times C, we now have 3 times 2.18:
      <md>
        3(2.18) = 6.54
      </md>
      The total cost is $6.54
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      In the previous episode, for a student with exam scores 80%, 90%, and an unknown score <m>x</m>, the expression representing their average score was:
      <md>
        \frac{80\% + 90\% + x}{3}
      </md>
      The student takes their third exam and earns a score of 72%. Use this info to complete the calculation in the expression.
    </p>
    <p>
      Replace the <m>x</m> in the formula with 72\%:
      <md>
        \frac{80\%+90\%+72\%}{3} = 80.666666666...\% \approx 80.67\%
      </md>
      Their exam average is 80.67%.
    </p> 
    </example>
    <p>
      <alert>Be particularly careful when doing evaluation with negative numbers.</alert>
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        Given that <m>x=-3</m>, evaluate the expression:
        <md>
          x^2
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        The expression is asking us to square <m>x</m>. Since the value of <m>x</m> is -3, we need to square -3. 
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>It would not be correct to write <m>-3^2</m>, </alert>, because this notation only squares the 3, note the minus sign. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Instead, we should wrap the -3 in parentheses: 
        <md>
          (-3)^2 = (-3)\times(-3) = 9
        </md>
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Given that <m>x=-2</m>, evaluate the expression:
        <md>
          5-x
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        It would not be correct to write <m>5 - -2</m>, since we should not have two minus/negative signs directly next to each other. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Instead, we should wrap the -2 in parentheses:
        <md>
          5-(-2) = 5+2 = 7
        </md>
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Given that <m>x=-4</m>, evaluate the expression:
        <md>
          3x
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Solution: It would not be correct to write 3-4, since this notation means “subtract 3 and 4”, but we want “multiply 3 by negative 4.” 
      </p>
      <p>
        Instead, we should wrap the -4 in parentheses:
        <md>
          3(-4) = -12
        </md>
      </p>
    </example>
    <assemblage>
      <title>Be Careful!</title>
      <p>
        Whenever you’re doing an evaluation problem with a negative value, it is recommended to wrap the negative number in parentheses.
      </p>
    </assemblage>
    <p>
      If a variable appears in a formula multiple times, you need to replace EVERY instance of that variable with its value.
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        Given that <m>x=2</m>, evaluate the expression
        <md>
          x^2 + 3x + 1
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Solution: Replace every <m>x</m> in the expression with 2:
        <md>
          2^2 + 3(2) + 1
        </md>
        <md>
          4+6+1 = 11
        </md>
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Formulas can contain two or more different variables. 
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        Given that <m>L=5</m> and <m>W = 7</m>, evaluate the formula:
        <md>
          2L + 2+W
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Solution: Replace every <m>L</m> with 5 and <m>W</m> with 7:
        <md>
          <mrow> 2(5)+2(7)\amp = 10+14 </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp = 24 </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </example>
    <worksheet xml:id="ws-s05e02">
      <title>Episode 2 Practice Problems</title>
      <p>
        Evaluate the following expressions at the given values:
      </p>
      <exercise workspace="0.5in">
        <p>
          Evaluate <m>3x+5</m> when <m>x=4</m>.
        </p>
      </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="0.5in">
        <p>
          Evaluate <m>2a^2-3a+7</m> when <m>a=2</m>.
        </p>
      </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="0.5in">
        <p>
          Evaluate <m>5y-2z</m> when <m>y=6</m> and <m>z=3</m>.
        </p>
      </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="0.5in">
        <p>
          Evaluate <m>x^3-4x</m> when <m>x=2</m>.
        </p>
      </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="0.5in">
        <p>
          Evaluate <m>4m^2+3n</m> when <m>m=1</m> and <m>n=5</m>.
        </p>
      </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="0.5in">
        <p>
          Evaluate <m>2(p+3)-5</m> when <m>p=7</m>.
        </p>
      </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="0.5in">
        <p>
          Evaluate <m>6x-x^2+4</m> when <m>x=3</m>.
        </p>
      </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="0.5in">
        <p>
          Evaluate <m>\frac{1}{4}a+b-5</m> when <m>a=2</m> and <m>b=\frac{1}{3}</m>.
        </p>
      </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="0.5in">
        <p>
          Evaluate <m>2(x-1)^2+3</m> when <m>x=5</m>.
        </p>
      </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="0.5in">
        <p>
          Evaluate <m>7y^2-2y+1</m> when <m>y=-1</m>.
        </p>
      </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="2.0in">
        <p>
          If a car travels at a speed R, for a length of time T, the distance they travel is given by the formula
          <md>
            R \times T
          </md>
          If the car’s speed is 40 miles per hour, and they drive for 1.4 hours, find the total distance traveled.
          </p>
      </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="2.0in">
        <p>
          If the temperature in the room is C degrees Celsius, then the temperature in degrees Fahrenheit is
          <md>
            \frac{9}{5}C+32
          </md>
          If the temperature in the room is 27 degrees Celsius, find the temperature in Fahrenheit.
          </p>
      </exercise>
    </worksheet>
</section>  